00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:51.870
is so universal that if one person walks up to another person and says, knock-knock,
它如此普及，以至于一个人走到另一个人面前说，敲门敲门，

324
00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:53.870
you just know to say, who's there?
你自然就知道该说，谁呀？

325
00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:54.370
Yeah.
是的。

326
00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.190
And then you know you're in a knock-knock joke.
然后你就知道你们正在进行一个敲门笑话。

327
00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:56.990
How did that begin?
这究竟是怎么开始的？

328
00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:57.990
Well.
嗯。

329
00:13:58.190 --> 00:13:58.650
Uh-oh.
噢哟。

330
00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.670
Somebody hit the Google button.
有人按谷歌搜索键了。

331
00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:01.330
Yes.
没错。

332
00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:05.350
As the lady who Googles, I'm going to give you the history of knock-knock jokes.
作为这个负责谷歌搜索的女士，我来给你们讲讲敲门笑话的历史。

333
00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:06.190
I want to hear it.
我很想听听。

334
00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:13.950
So the first written coupling of knock-knock, who's there, the first time it sort of appeared
那么，敲门、谁呀这种形式的文字结合，据人们所知第一次出现在任何地方，

335
00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.970
anywhere, people believe, is Shakespeare's Macbeth.
是莎士比亚的《麦克白》。

336
00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.230
All roads lead back to Shakespeare.
条条大路通莎士比亚。

337
00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:22.310
That's why it was so important to study it in theater school.
这就是为什么在戏剧学院学这个那么重要。

338
00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:24.510
Hey, I studied Shakespeare.
嘿，我学过莎士比亚。

339
00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:25.810
I believe you.
我相信你。

340
00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:27.770
But I studied it in my writing class.
不过我是从写作课上学的。

341
00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:29.550
Well, there you go.
好吧，那就对了。

342
00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:29.890
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

343
00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:38.010
Act two, scene three, the porter is very hungover, and he has this monologue where he repeats,
第二幕第三场，门房宿醉得很厉害，他有一段独白，里面反复念叨着

344
00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.290
knock-knock, who's there?
敲门敲门，谁呀？

345
00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.030
And then he tells you who's there.
然后他自己说出是谁在敲门。

346
00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:43.770
He's sort of just kind of in his head.
他当时有点神志不清。

347
00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.590
Tis I, a Montague!
正是我，一个蒙太古！

348
00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.710
But in this play, it is not a joke.
但在剧中，这并不是一个笑话。

349
00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.270
He's not doing, like, a jokey thing.
他不是在开玩笑。

350
00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:55.490
It's not like what it is now.
跟现在的性质不一样。

351
00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.290
So now, cut to, it's the early 1900s.
所以现在，镜头一转，那是20世纪初。

352
00:14:59.350 --> 00:14:59.590
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

353
00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.570
And there's this new kind of comedy on the scene.
当时出现了一种新颖的喜剧。

354
00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:03.730
Ooh, what kind?
哦，什么类型的？

355
00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:08.010
It's like a call-answer comedy or like a bait-and-response comedy.
有点像一问一答式的喜剧，或者像诱导回应式的喜剧。

356
00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.790
And here's an example of one of these jokes from the 1900s.
这里有一个20世纪这种笑话的例子。

357
00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:14.750
Someone would say, do you know Arthur?
有人会说，你认识亚瑟吗？

358
00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.830
And that would make the other person say, Arthur who?
然后另一个人会说，哪个亚瑟？

359
00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:18.410
Mm.
嗯。

360
00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:20.590
And then you would say, our thermometer.
接着你会说，咱们的温度计（Our thermometer，谐音Arthur thermometer）。

361
00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:22.010
Who?
谁？

362
00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:23.450
Yes.
对。

363
00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.110
That was one of the jokes.
这就是其中一个笑话。

364
00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:26.410
My kids would love that one.
我的孩子们一定会喜欢这个。

365
00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:27.150
Saving it.
先记下了。

366
00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:30.150
Well, it's very similar to that whole up dog joke.
嗯，这和那个“什么狗狗”（up dog）的笑话非常相似。

367
00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:31.950
The what's up dog.
也就是“最近有什么新鲜事”（What's up dog）。

368
00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:32.290
Mm.
嗯。

369
00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:33.090
I don't know.
我不知道。

370
00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:33.890
What's up with you?
你最近在忙什么？

371
00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:34.590
Right.
对啊。

372
00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:35.090
Right?
对吧？

373
00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:37.070
That Michael famously botched.
迈克尔当年把这个搞砸了，这很有名。

374
00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:46.630
So then, in the mid-1930s, this kind of like pun mania morphed into what is now the
所以到了20世纪30年代中期，这种双关语狂热演变成了现在

375
00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.090
traditional knock-knock joke.
传统的敲门笑话。

376
00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:54.510
Cartoonist Bob Dunn is credited with inventing this joke in its current form.
漫画家鲍勃·邓恩被认为以目前的形式发明了这种笑话。

377
00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:57.650
He wrote a book called Knock Knock Who's There?
他写了一本书叫《敲敲门，谁呀？》

378
00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:02.610
And it's basically filled with knock-knock joke puns.
里面基本上全是敲门双关笑话。

379
00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:04.270
It sold like two million copies.
它卖出了大约两百万本。

380
00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.850
It took off like crazy.
它火得一塌糊涂。

381
00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:06.890
It was a Jeopardy!
它还成了《危险边缘！》

382
00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:07.390
question.
节目里的一道考题。

383
00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:08.910
Get this.
你听这个。

384
00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:09.590
What?
什么？

385
00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:13.450
Knock-knock jokes were especially huge in Pennsylvania.
敲门笑话在宾夕法尼亚州尤其火爆。

386
00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:14.750
Oh.
哦。

387
00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:18.950
Versions of knock-knock jokes started appearing in advertisements.
敲门笑话的各种版本开始出现在广告中。

388
00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:25.030
The Edgemont Cash and Carry grocery store in Chester, Pennsylvania ran an ad that said,
宾夕法尼亚州切斯特市的埃奇芒特现购自运杂货店登过一条广告，上面写着

389
00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:26.690
Knock-knock, who's there?
敲敲门，谁呀？

390
00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:27.490
Don.
唐（Don）。

391
00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:28.590
Don who?
哪个唐？

392
00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:31.890
Don forget to do your shopping at the Cash and Carry.
别（Don't，谐音Don）忘了去现购自运商店买东西。

393
00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:34.150
Yeah.
对。

394
00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:35.970
Don forget it.
别忘了。

395
00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:37.810
There was also this one.
还有这样一个。

396
00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:38.050
Ready?
准备好了吗？

397
00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:39.490
Knock-knock.
敲敲门。

398
00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:40.210
Who's there?
谁呀？

399
00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:40.930
Rufus.
鲁弗斯（Rufus）。

400
00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:41.730
Rufus who?
哪个鲁弗斯？

401
00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.230
Roof is the most important part of your house.
屋顶是你房子最重要的部分。

402
00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:46.410
That was a roofing company.
那是一家屋顶工程公司。

403
00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:46.990
Okay.
好的。

404
00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.650
But politicians started using them.
但政客们开始利用它们。

405
00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:59.070
There were like special clubs called knock-knock clubs that formed in Illinois and Iowa and Kansas.
当时伊利诺伊州、艾奥瓦州和堪萨斯州出现了一些被称为“咚咚俱乐部”的特殊俱乐部。

406
00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:02.030
And I guess knock-knock clubs?
我想就是“咚咚俱乐部”？

407
00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:03.290
You would just go there.
你只要去那儿就行。

408
00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.830
And tell your knock-knock jokes?
然后讲你们的敲门笑话？

409
00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:10.830
Yes, it was like a place to have a drink and share your greatest knock-knock jokes.
对，那就像是个可以喝一杯并分享你最棒的敲门笑话的地方。

410
00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:21.130
Then, in 1936, at like the peak of the knock-knock joke craze, there was a song called The Knock-Knock Song by Fletcher Henderson.
然后，在1936年，大约是在敲门笑话狂热的巅峰时期，有一首由弗莱彻·亨德森演唱的歌叫《敲门之歌》。

411
00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:22.690
And I'm going to play you a clip.
我要放一段音频给你听。

412
00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:23.790
Oh, my word.
哦，天哪。

413
00:17:27.320 --> 00:17:31.800
Many, many, many, many thanks to you.
非常、非常、非常、非常感谢你。

414
00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:44.430
Yeah, I love it.
对，我很喜欢。

415
00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:46.410
A whole song full of knock-knock jokes.
整首歌都是满满的敲门笑话。

416
00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:50.470
So then, lady, there was a backlash.
所以后来，女士，出现了强烈的反弹。

417
00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.330
Oh, people turn on the knock-knock?
哦，人们开始反感敲门笑话了吗？

418
00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:59.130
Well, all of these psychologists start coming out, like Sigmund Freud and A.A.
嗯，所有这些心理学家开始出现，比如西格蒙德·弗洛伊德和A.A.

419
00:17:59.250 --> 00:17:59.410
Brill.
布里尔。

420
00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:06.810
They start saying that people who have an incessant need to make puns have a psychological condition.
他们开始说，那些有不断说双关语需求的人患有某种心理疾病。

421
00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:07.490
Oh, no.
哦，不。

422
00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.130
That it is like a sign of narcissism.
这就像是自恋的征兆。

423
00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:10.690
Oh.
哦。

424
00:18:10.690 --> 00:18:22.010
A German neurologist named Otfried Forster said manic punning was a certifiable syndrome, and he actually called it Forster syndrome.
一个名叫奥特弗里德·福斯特的德国神经学家说狂躁地说双关语是一种确诊的综合征，他甚至把它叫做福斯特综合征。

425
00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.070
He named it after himself.
他用自己的名字命名了它。

426
00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:24.970
I mean.
我的意思是。

427
00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:27.070
Aren't you being a narcissist, Mr. Forster?
福斯特先生，你难道不是很自恋吗？

428
00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:28.650
I mean, I don't know.
我是说，我不知道。

429
00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:33.470
You're telling me that people that love and pun are narcissists, and then you name it after yourself.
你告诉我喜欢说双关语的人都是自恋狂，然后你却用自己的名字来命名它。

430
00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:34.110
That's right.
没错。

431
00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.250
I just want to point that out.
我只是想指出这一点。

432
00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:36.030
Yeah.
对。

433
00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:40.690
Well, despite their best efforts, knock-knock jokes have stayed around.
好吧，尽管他们尽了最大努力，敲门笑话还是流传了下来。

434
00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.750
